From 336d98a1f23324f49654758cd9dcb21ceebcb5f0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "A. Reader" <reader@example.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 05:36:41 +0530
Subject: [PATCH bpf-next 3/8] bpf: Add count-min sketch map type

A count-min sketch counts any number of keys in memory fixed at
creation: depth rows of width counters, one hash function per row. An
update adds to one counter in each row and a query returns the smallest
of the key's counters. The estimate is never below the true count, and
with width = ceil(e / epsilon) and depth = ceil(ln(1 / delta)) it
exceeds it by more than epsilon times the total of all counts with
probability at most delta (Cormode and Muthukrishnan, 2005).

A program that counts events per thread, flow or address in a hash map
needs an entry per key; the map fills (-E2BIG) or, as an LRU hash,
forgets keys. The sketch keeps an estimate for every key in
8 * width * depth bytes.

The map is keyless, like the bloom filter, and uses the same helpers:
the value is the key's bytes followed by a u64 count, push adds the
count to the key, and peek returns the key's estimate. From user space,
BPF_MAP_UPDATE_ELEM pushes and BPF_MAP_LOOKUP_ELEM writes the estimate
into the value's count. max_entries is the width and the lower 4 bits
of map_extra the depth (default 5). Counters are updated with atomic
adds: a key such as a thread ID is written from one CPU at a time, so
two CPUs meet on a counter only when their keys collide in a row.

Like a bloom filter peek, a count-min peek reads its value, which
stack liveness must count as a read. The map is allowed in sleepable
programs: push and peek touch only the
map's counters.
